# `actuarial::models::claim_cost`

Expected claim cost from exposure, frequency and severity.

## Signature

```cpp
pluss::Series actuarial::models::claim_cost(const pluss::Series &exposure, const pluss::Series &claim_frequency, const pluss::Series &claim_severity);
```

## What It Does

Returns {math}`\text{exposure}\times\text{frequency}\times\text{severity}`, elementwise: the expected total claims of a rating cell is its volume times the claims per unit of volume times the average claim. The three arguments broadcast, so a single severity can be combined with a vector of cells. The product of frequency and severity is the pure premium per unit of exposure; multiplied by exposure it is the expected loss. Use it to build an expected loss from assumptions, and [`pure_premium`](pure_premium.md) for the reverse step from observed losses.

## Parameters

`exposure`
: Volume of each cell, finite and 0 or more.

`claim_frequency`
: Claims per unit of exposure, finite and 0 or more.

`claim_severity`
: Average amount of a claim, finite and 0 or more.

## Returns

A series of the broadcast shape of the inputs.

## Errors

Throws `<argument> must be defined`, `<argument> must be finite` and `<argument> must be >= 0`, prefixed with the function name for each argument (`exposure`, `claim_frequency`, `claim_severity`), and `broadcast: incompatible shapes` when the shapes do not broadcast.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1: three cells

Exposure, frequency and severity per cell.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(1));
  const pluss::Series exposure = pluss::make_series({1000.0, 1200.0, 1500.0});
  const pluss::Series frequency = pluss::make_series({0.08, 0.10, 0.07});
  const pluss::Series severity = pluss::make_series({5000.0, 4800.0, 5200.0});
  out.writeln(actuarial::models::claim_cost(exposure, frequency, severity).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 2: by hand

Cell 0: 1,000 x 0.08 x 5,000.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(1));
  out.writeln(1000.0 * 0.08 * 5000.0, actuarial::models::claim_cost(pluss::make_series({1000.0}), pluss::make_series({0.08}), pluss::make_series({5000.0})).items<double>()[0]);
}
```

### Example 3: one severity for every cell

A single severity broadcasts across the cells.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(1));
  out.writeln(actuarial::models::claim_cost(pluss::make_series({1000.0, 1200.0, 1500.0}), pluss::make_series({0.08, 0.10, 0.07}), pluss::make_series({5000.0})).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 4: back to a pure premium

Dividing the cost by the exposure recovers frequency times severity, which is what pure_premium measures from data.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(1));
  const pluss::Series exposure = pluss::make_series({1000.0, 1200.0, 1500.0});
  const pluss::Series cost = actuarial::models::claim_cost(exposure, pluss::make_series({0.08, 0.10, 0.07}), pluss::make_series({5000.0, 4800.0, 5200.0}));
  out.writeln(cost.div(exposure).items<double>());
  out.writeln(actuarial::models::premium_curve(cost, exposure).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 5: a cell with no exposure

Zero exposure gives zero cost.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(1));
  out.writeln(actuarial::models::claim_cost(pluss::make_series({0.0, 1000.0}), pluss::make_series({0.08, 0.08}), pluss::make_series({5000.0, 5000.0})).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 6: the errors

Negative and non-finite inputs and shapes that do not broadcast are refused.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const auto report = [&](const auto &call) {
    try {
      call();
    } catch (const std::exception &e) {
      std::string text(e.what());
      if (const auto cut = text.find("Triggered"); cut != std::string::npos) text.erase(cut);
      while (!text.empty() && text.back() == '\n') text.pop_back();
      out.writeln(text);
    }
  };
  report([&] { (void)actuarial::models::claim_cost(pluss::make_series({-1.0}), pluss::make_series({0.1}), pluss::make_series({100.0})); });
  report([&] { (void)actuarial::models::claim_cost(pluss::make_series({10.0}), pluss::make_series({std::nan("")}), pluss::make_series({100.0})); });
  report([&] { (void)actuarial::models::claim_cost(pluss::series::undefined(), pluss::make_series({0.1}), pluss::make_series({100.0})); });
  report([&] { (void)actuarial::models::claim_cost(pluss::make_series({1.0, 2.0}), pluss::make_series({0.1, 0.2, 0.3}), pluss::make_series({100.0})); });
}
```

